Relevant columns, their potential usage and likely conditions

pizza_customer_orders  
• order_id: unique identifier for each order; join‑key to runner and extras/exclusions helper tables.  
• customer_id: needed for the requested output.  
• pizza_id: maps every order to a pizza type (1 = Meatlovers, 2 = Vegetarian in the sample – the task finally wants every non‑1 pizza shown with id 2).  
• exclusions / extras: comma‑separated topping_id values that have to be removed / added.  We will often check WHERE exclusions IS NOT NULL  (or … <> '') and the same for extras.  
• order_time: appears in the GROUP BY clause the final task describes.  

pizza_names  
• pizza_id: join‑key to recipes / orders.  
• pizza_name: the human‑readable name to be shown in the output.  

pizza_recipes  
• pizza_id: join‑key to names / orders.  
• toppings: comma‑separated list of standard topping_id values that form the base recipe.  

pizza_toppings  
• topping_id: join‑key to recipes / extras / exclusions.  
• topping_name: the readable ingredient; will finally be alphabetically concatenated.  

pizza_get_exclusions  
• row_id: helper “row id” that the task asks us to report; together with order_id it tells which exclusion row belongs to which order.  
• order_id / exclusions: already one topping_id per row (plus total_exclusions count) – handy for splitting.  

pizza_get_extras  
• row_id: analogous to the previous table, but for extras that must be added (may appear multiple times per order).  
• order_id / extras / extras_count: one topping_id per row and how many times it was requested (needed for 2x prefix logic).  

Typical conditions / joins we will use  
• JOIN pizza_names     USING (pizza_id)  
• JOIN pizza_recipes   USING (pizza_id)  
• JOIN pizza_toppings  ON pizza_toppings.topping_id = …  
• LEFT JOIN pizza_get_exclusions e  ON e.order_id = o.order_id  
• LEFT JOIN pizza_get_extras     x  ON x.order_id = o.order_id  
• WHERE o.pizza_id = 1  -- Meatlovers only (example)  
• WHERE o.exclusions IS NOT NULL AND o.exclusions <> ''  

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (all limited to 20 rows) that progressively move from simple inspection to a nearly complete answer.  
They are purely SELECT statements, use DISTINCT where useful, and each is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1) List all pizza names to see the mapping between pizza_id and pizza_name
SELECT DISTINCT pizza_id, pizza_name
FROM pizza_names
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) What pizza types actually appear in customer orders?
SELECT DISTINCT pizza_id
FROM pizza_customer_orders
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Inspect the raw 'toppings' recipe strings for every pizza_id
SELECT pizza_id, toppings
FROM pizza_recipes
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Show the topping_id → topping_name lookup table
SELECT topping_id, topping_name
FROM pizza_toppings
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Grab orders that have at least one exclusion typed directly in pizza_customer_orders
SELECT order_id, exclusions
FROM pizza_customer_orders
WHERE exclusions IS NOT NULL AND exclusions <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Review the pre‑split exclusions per order in helper table pizza_get_exclusions
SELECT row_id, order_id, exclusions AS topping_id, total_exclusions
FROM pizza_get_exclusions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Review the pre‑split extras per order in helper table pizza_get_extras
SELECT row_id, order_id, extras  AS topping_id, extras_count
FROM pizza_get_extras
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Join an order to its pizza name and show any direct (raw‑string) extras / exclusions
SELECT o.order_id,
       o.customer_id,
       n.pizza_name,
       o.extras,
       o.exclusions,
       o.order_time
FROM   pizza_customer_orders AS o
JOIN   pizza_names           AS n  USING (pizza_id)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For a single order, resolve the standard recipe into readable topping names
SELECT o.order_id,
       GROUP_CONCAT(t.topping_name, ', ') AS standard_recipe
FROM   pizza_customer_orders AS o
JOIN   pizza_recipes         AS r  USING (pizza_id)
JOIN   pizza_toppings        AS t  ON t.topping_id IN (
                                          SELECT TRIM(value)
                                          FROM   json_each('[' || r.toppings || ']')
                                   )
WHERE  o.order_id = 1
GROUP  BY o.order_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) (Toward final answer) Produce one row per order with row_id from extras/exclusions helper,
--     mapping pizza name and a preliminary ingredient list (still without 2x logic)
SELECT e.row_id,
       o.order_id,
       o.customer_id,
       n.pizza_name,
       GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT t.topping_name, ', ') AS ingredients  -- preliminary
FROM   pizza_customer_orders AS o
JOIN   pizza_names            AS n  USING (pizza_id)
JOIN   pizza_recipes          AS r  USING (pizza_id)
JOIN   pizza_get_exclusions   AS e  ON e.order_id = o.order_id
JOIN   pizza_toppings         AS t  ON t.topping_id IN (
                                           SELECT TRIM(value)
                                           FROM   json_each('[' || r.toppings || ']')
                                      )
GROUP  BY e.row_id, o.order_id, o.customer_id, n.pizza_name
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries build familiarity with the key tables and values needed to craft the complete solution that removes excluded toppings, adds extras (possibly repeating them), alphabetises the final list, prefixes “2x” where necessary, and concatenates everything after the pizza name.
