```sql
--Description / thinking process:
-- 1. Purpose: demonstrate how to fix the “DISTINCT aggregates must have exactly one
--    argument” error that occurred when we tried to supply a custom separator in
--    GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT …, ', ').
-- 2. Rule: in SQLite, GROUP_CONCAT with DISTINCT can accept only ONE argument, so we
--    must drop the second “delimiter” parameter.
-- 3. Strategy:
--      • Start from pizza_customer_orders (o) because every order lives there.
--      • Join pizza_names (n) to obtain the readable pizza_name.
--      • Join pizza_recipes (r) for the comma‑separated topping_id list that defines
--        the standard recipe.
--      • Use json_each on '[' || r.toppings || ']' to turn that list into rows.
--      • Join pizza_toppings (t) to translate topping_id → topping_name.
--      • Join pizza_get_exclusions (e) ONLY to pull in a row_id; we are not yet
--        filtering by exclusions in this quick repair, but the join lets each helper
--        row appear once, which was the original goal of query #10.
--      • GROUP BY that row_id (plus order_id, customer_id, pizza_name) and
--        concatenate DISTINCT topping_name values; no second argument supplied, so
--        SQLite will use its default comma separator.
-- 4. LIMIT 20 to keep the result set small during exploration.
SELECT
    e.row_id,
    o.order_id,
    o.customer_id,
    n.pizza_name,
    GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT t.topping_name) AS ingredients
FROM   pizza_customer_orders AS o
JOIN   pizza_names          AS n  USING (pizza_id)
JOIN   pizza_recipes        AS r  USING (pizza_id)
JOIN   pizza_get_exclusions AS e  ON e.order_id = o.order_id
JOIN   pizza_toppings       AS t
       ON t.topping_id IN (
            SELECT CAST(TRIM(value) AS INTEGER)
            FROM   json_each('[' || r.toppings || ']')
       )
GROUP BY e.row_id,
         o.order_id,
         o.customer_id,
         n.pizza_name
LIMIT 20;
```
